 Silver Proof coin. Die trial pieces are coin‑like strikes produced in very small numbers by our engineers and production specialists to test new designs and processes before a coin is released to the public. Serving as working tools within our factory, they help ensure that every element of the approved artwork translates faithfully from a two‑dimensional design into a finished piece that meets our exacting standards of craftsmanship.​ Once the Lunar Year of the Rabbit concept and designs had been approved, our engineering team used trial pieces such as this to refine the striking parameters, study the finish and relief on a large one‑kilogram canvas, and confirm that the intricate detail of the rabbit motif and associated artistry could be rendered with clarity and depth. Throughout this phase, only a very limited number of die trial pieces are produced, often across several experiments, and most are subsequently destroyed once the final specification is agreed. Only a small handful are retained in our museum collection as reference for future projects, which underscores the rarity of any example released to collectors.​ As a one‑kilogram Silver Proof trial, this piece reflects the finished aesthetic of the Lunar Year of the Rabbit coin, with a highly polished field and carefully controlled frosting designed to accentuate every engraved detail. The large diameter provides an impressive display of the rabbit design, while also revealing the subtle technical refinements that occur during testing, from pressure adjustments to the balance between mirror‑like surfaces and frosted devices. For specialists, these nuances offer a tangible record of the journey from concept to approved production piece.​ Die trial pieces have been part of The Royal Mint’s practice for centuries, and examples from the eighteenth century onwards are preserved in The Royal Mint Museum as evidence of changing techniques and artistic approaches. Modern die trial pieces, such as this Lunar Year 